# Deployment parameters for this rig — the single source of truth.
#
# Read by: server.py (via python-dotenv), mcp-run.sh (which launches the MCP server),
# the Makefile (via -include), and set_env.sh. Change a value here and every one of
# them follows. A real environment variable always wins over anything set here, so
# `GOOGLE_CLOUD_ZONE=us-central1-b make status` still works for a one-off.
#
# THIS RIG PROVISIONS THROUGH COMPUTE ENGINE, NOT THE CLOUD TPU API. Forked from
# gce-vllm-v6e8-2b on 2026-08-25 and retargeted from E2B to the 31B checkpoint. Same eight-chip
# v6e slice, same control plane, same code lineage — the CHECKPOINT is the only thing that
# changed, and every value below that depends on it has been rederived. See CLAUDE.md and
# SERVING-PARAMS.md.
#
# IT IS NOT THE A/B TWIN OF ANYTHING. Its parent gce-vllm-v6e8-2b pairs with tpu-vllm-v6e8-2b
# as a control-plane comparison; this rig has no counterpart on the TPU API, because no rig
# here serves 31B through it. A measurement from here is a 31B-on-v6e-8 measurement and
# nothing else. Do NOT diff it against a 2B rig on either control plane and call the delta a
# control-plane result — the checkpoint dominates every number by more than an order of
# magnitude.
#
# NOTHING HERE HAS RUN. This rig has provisioned nothing and measured nothing. Every figure
# below is arithmetic from `@../MODELS.md` and `@../HARDWARE.md`, and is labelled as such.
#
# Zone note: europe-west4-a. BOTH europe-west4-a and us-east5-b publish ct6e-standard-8t —
# machine-type availability is not the discriminator. The discriminator is Compute Engine
# quota, which is regional, and which this project holds in europe-west4 (CT6E=32) and not
# in us-central1/us-east4/us-west1 (unset, which reads as 0). The 512 v6e chips this project
# holds in us-east5 are TPU API quota and are unreachable from this path.
#
# europe-west4-a granted a flex-start ct6e-standard-1t immediately on 2026-08-10, with no
# DWS queueing. THAT IS NOT EVIDENCE ABOUT AN EIGHT-CHIP REQUEST: it is a different unit of
# capacity, and quota is a ceiling rather than an allocation. Probe with SPOT, which fails
# fast with `reason: stockout`, before committing a flex-start that merely queues.

GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=aisprint-491218
GOOGLE_CLOUD_REGION=europe-west4
GOOGLE_CLOUD_ZONE=europe-west4-a

# The reference bf16 instruction-tuned 31B release — no weight encoding, which is why the
# directory carries no slot-5 suffix (`@../NAMING.md`). 31.0B parameters, 62 GB on disk at
# bf16, 57.7 GiB resident, ~7.2 GiB per chip at TENSOR_PARALLEL_SIZE=8. That fits the slice
# with room to spare; the constraint on this checkpoint is KV, not weights — see MAX_MODEL_LEN.
#
# The QAT exports `-qat-w4a16-ct` and `-q4_0-unquantized` both exist for this size and neither
# is reachable from here: `@../QUANTIZATION.md` records no bootable weight-quantization route
# on the vLLM/tpu_inference JAX path for Gemma 4. A w4a16 31B rig would be a different
# directory (gce-vllm-v6e8-31b-w4a16), not a flag change in this one.
MODEL_NAME=google/gemma-4-31B-it

# --- What Compute Engine actually consumes -----------------------------------------------
# MACHINE_TYPE is the real accelerator request on this path. ct6e-standard-8t is EIGHT v6e
# chips on ONE host, 360 vCPU / 1440 GB, published in the same 19 zones as the 1-chip shape
# (verified 2026-08-19). Single host means every collective travels the on-board ICI; no
# data-centre network is involved and no multi-host coordination is needed.
#
# There is a second family spelled <name>-tpu, identical in vCPU, memory and zone coverage
# but reporting guestAcceleratorType tpu-v6e instead of ct6e; the two are not known to be
# interchangeable, so this is config rather than a derived string. Do not read the bare form
# as legacy — Google's own CE quickstart creates ct6e-standard-4t, bare.
MACHINE_TYPE=ct6e-standard-8t
# Chips in one instance of MACHINE_TYPE. server.py derives this from the trailing `-<N>t` of
# the machine type and only reads this variable as an override; set it if a future shape
# stops encoding the count in its name. It drives the cost estimate and the quota arithmetic,
# both of which are 8x what they were on the single-chip fork.
CHIP_COUNT=8
# Mesh shape of the slice, for estimate_deployment_cost and for reading traces. v6e-8 is 2x4.
TOPOLOGY=2x4
# Replaces the TPU API's --runtime-version. Pin the FAMILY, never a dated build: images ship
# roughly weekly and every superseded build goes DEPRECATED within the fortnight.
IMAGE_FAMILY=ubuntu-accel-2204-amd64-tpu-v5e-v5p-v6e
IMAGE_PROJECT=ubuntu-os-accelerator-images
# The image default is 10GB, which cannot hold the vLLM TPU image. Undersizing fails late,
# after boot, during the docker pull.
BOOT_DISK_SIZE_GB=200

# ACCELERATOR_TYPE is documentation here — the Cloud TPU API's spelling, kept so benchmark
# reports and the directory name line up with the twin rig. NEVER passed to gcloud on
# this path; `gcloud compute instances create` would reject it.
ACCELERATOR_TYPE=v6e-8

# --- Tensor parallelism, and the reason this number deserves a second look -----------------
# 8 uses the whole slice. On THIS checkpoint that is a real win, which is the OPPOSITE of what
# the parent rig's copy of this comment said — do not carry E2B's reasoning over.
#
# The KV cache shards across KV heads, not weight columns, so a layer's cache shards
# min(TP, kv_heads) ways and the runtime pads the head count up to TP when it falls short.
# `@../MODELS.md` gives the 31B two attention geometries and they land on opposite sides of 8:
#
#   50 sliding layers   num_key_value_heads = 16        16 >= 8, shards 2 heads/chip exactly
#   10 full layers      num_global_key_value_heads = 4   4 <  8, PADS to 8 — 2x aggregate KV
#
# So TP=8 costs a 2x KV penalty on ten of sixty layers and nothing on the other fifty: 960 KiB
# per token against an ideal 880 (arithmetic under MAX_MODEL_LEN below). A 9.1% overhead, not
# E2B's 8x. E2B was the degenerate case — num_key_value_heads=1, every layer at the limit.
#
# Zimbres 2026 (When_TP_Crosses_the_KV_Head_Count_v6e8.pdf, in this directory) measures this
# crossing on THIS checkpoint and THIS machine type: layers whose KV heads still shard at TP=8
# sped up 1.92x, layers at the head limit only 1.26x. The paper stopped being an analogy when
# the checkpoint changed — it is about the model this rig now serves.
#
# TP=8 is also not optional here the way it was for E2B. 57.7 GiB of weights do not fit one
# 31.24 GiB chip, so TP=1 and TP=2 CANNOT BOOT this checkpoint; TP=4 is the only alternative
# worth sweeping, and it halves the KV pool. The parent rig's instruction to sweep 1/2/4/8 is
# not runnable here — sweep 4 vs 8 before treating 8 as settled.
TENSOR_PARALLEL_SIZE=8

# --- Serving parameters. See SERVING-PARAMS.md for the decision and its evidence. ----------
#
# KV ARITHMETIC FOR THIS CHECKPOINT, because it is 53x the parent rig's and every capacity
# question here reduces to it. Derived from `@../MODELS.md`, bf16, uncapped sliding window
# (tpu_inference sets disable_sliding_window for every Gemma 4 size — the 256/512 head-dim
# split trips it family-wide — so the 50 sliding layers allocate full length even though they
# never read past 1024 tokens):
#
#   50 sliding x 16 heads x 256 head_dim  x 2 planes x 2 B = 819,200 B
#   10 full    x  4 heads x 512 global_hd x 2 planes x 2 B =  81,920 B
#                                                    ideal = 901,120 B = 880 KiB/token
#   at TP=8 the 10 full layers pad 4 heads -> 8     actual = 983,040 B = 960 KiB/token
#
# Against E2B's 18 KiB/token. Budget on the slice: 8 x 31.24 GiB usable = 249.9 GiB, less
# 57.7 GiB of weights, less ~19.8 GiB of headroom (the v6e-1 measured 2.48 GiB, x8) leaves a
# KV pool near 172 GiB and roughly 184,000 KV tokens. One 32,768-token sequence claims 30 GiB
# of that. ALL OF THIS IS ARITHMETIC — no allocation log has ever been read on this rig, and
# `@../MODELS.md` is explicit that a 12B rig's published per-token figure was wrong twice over
# by mixing a windowed rate with an unwindowed one. Confirm against the boot log's
# total_hbm_used_gb before quoting any of it.
#
# MAX_MODEL_LEN is a PER-REQUEST CEILING, not an allocation, so 32768 does not cost
# concurrency on shorter requests — the pool is shared and the scheduler admission-controls
# rather than evicts (measured on the parent rig: zero preemptions at 157% of pool). It does
# bound the worst case at ~5 concurrent full-length sequences. 32768 is kept because it is the
# parent rig's value and one sequence fits the pool six times over.
MAX_MODEL_LEN=32768
# Bounded below by --disable_chunked_mm_input, which requires one multimodal item to fit in a
# single batch. UNVERIFIED FOR THIS CHECKPOINT: 4096 clears E2B's 2,496-token item, and the
# 31B's vision tower is a different size that nobody here has counted. If it is larger, vLLM
# REFUSES TO START and names the required floor in the error — read it out of the boot log
# rather than guessing, then raise this to the next power of two.
MAX_NUM_BATCHED_TOKENS=4096

# --- Checkpoint staging: the cheapest change in this rig ------------------------------------
# Empty means "pull the 62 GB checkpoint from Hugging Face at boot", which is what this rig
# has always done and what the 90-minute boot-readiness budget in startup_script_template.sh
# is sized for. AT $10.80/hr FOR EIGHT CHIPS THAT PULL IS ~$16 OF EVERY BOOT, spent before a
# single token is served, and it is spent again on every fresh instance.
#
# Point this at a staged tar of the Hugging Face cache in a bucket in GOOGLE_CLOUD_REGION and
# the boot restores it in-region instead — minutes, not an hour. `./stage_model_to_gcs.sh`
# creates the bucket, grants the TPU service account read, and stages the object on a cheap
# CPU VM that deletes itself; `--status` says whether it is done.
#
# It is an OPTIMIZATION ONLY. The startup script checks the object is readable and falls back
# to the online pull on any failure, so a wrong value here costs boot time, never a boot.
# The region has to match: a bucket outside GOOGLE_CLOUD_REGION restores over the public
# backbone and gives back the entire saving.
# Staged 2026-08-25: 62,578,739,200 B (58.28 GiB). Download 311s, upload 1093s on one
# e2-standard-8 in europe-west4-a. The object is 58.28 GiB against 59 GiB of on-disk cache,
# which is the check that tar stored the snapshots/ symlinks as LINKS — dereferenced they
# would have doubled it.
MODEL_GCS_URI=gs://aisprint-491218-hf-cache-europe-west4/google__gemma-4-31B-it.hfcache.tar

# --- Provisioning -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# flex-start, spot, on-demand, or reservation-bound. These are this rig's lowercase labels;
# server.py maps them to gcloud's SCREAMING_CASE --provisioning-model values. Every creation
# tool takes a provisioning_model argument that overrides this per call.
#   flex-start        - FLEX_START. Queues for scarce capacity via Dynamic Workload
#                       Scheduler, then self-deletes at MAX_RUN_DURATION.
#   spot              - SPOT. Reclaimable with ~30s notice. In us-east5 v6e spot lists
#                       DEARER than flex-start; read estimate_deployment_cost, don't assume.
#   on-demand         - STANDARD. Full price, no preemption.
#   reservation-bound - RESERVATION_BOUND. Consumes a calendar or dense-deployment
#                       reservation. HAS NO QUEUED RESOURCE EQUIVALENT — only on this path.
#
# EIGHT CHIPS BILL AS EIGHT CHIPS. Every rate in the catalog is per chip-hour, so this rig
# costs 8x the single-chip fork for the same wall-clock: at the us-east5 v6e flex-start list
# price of $1.35/chip-hr that is $10.80/hr, and a 4h MAX_RUN_DURATION is a $43 instance.
# Run estimate_deployment_cost before a create; it reads live pricing and multiplies by
# CHIP_COUNT.
PROVISIONING_MODEL=flex-start
# How long to keep asking for flex-start capacity, and how long the VM may run once granted.
# Unlike the TPU API, --max-run-duration is not flex-start-only here: spot and on-demand get
# an automatic stop too, paired with --instance-termination-action=DELETE.
REQUEST_VALID_FOR=2h
MAX_RUN_DURATION=4h
# The reservation RESERVATION_BOUND consumes. Required for that model and ignored by the other
# three: gcloud accepts RESERVATION_BOUND only alongside --reservation-affinity=specific, so
# the model on its own is not a complete request. Empty because this project holds no future
# reservation yet. Create one out of band — it is a separate resource, not a create-time flag:
#   gcloud compute future-reservations create <name> --reservation-mode=CALENDAR \
#     --tpu-version=V6E --chip-count=8 --workload-type=SERVING --zone=europe-west4-a \
#     --start-time=<RFC3339> --duration=1d --planning-status=SUBMITTED \
#     --require-specific-reservation --reservation-name=<name>
# Note --chip-count=8, not 1: the reservation has to match the slice this rig asks for.
# Calendar mode is 1-90 days, visible 120 days ahead, and CANNOT be modified or cancelled once
# submitted. v6e calendar zones: asia-northeast1-b, europe-west4-a, us-east5-a/-b, us-south1-ai1b.
# A reservation is pinned to its zone, so GOOGLE_CLOUD_ZONE must match it.
RESERVATION_NAME=

# --- Quota: the trap this rig exists to document -------------------------------------------
# The two control planes meter against DIFFERENT POOLS. This project holds 512 v6e chips in
# us-east5 under the TPU API and NO STATED VALUE for family CT6E in us-central1, us-east4 or
# us-west1 under the Compute Engine quota this path consumes. TPU API quota does not carry over.
#
# It does NOT follow that the project has no Compute Engine v6e quota. Family CT6E, read
# 2026-08-19: 32 in asia-east1, asia-northeast1, asia-south1, asia-southeast1, europe-west4,
# southamerica-east1, southamerica-west1, us-east1, us-east5, us-south1; unset (reads as 0)
# in us-central1, us-east4, us-west1. The trap is that the pools are disjoint and regionally
# misaligned, not that one is empty.
#
# QUOTA IS COUNTED IN CHIPS, AND THIS RIG SPENDS EIGHT PER INSTANCE. That is the one number
# the retarget from v6e-1 changed: CT6E=32 in europe-west4 was 32 concurrent single-chip
# instances and is 4 concurrent eight-chip ones. us-east5's preemptible 32 — which every
# other live region holds at 1536, and which was denied on re-request — is likewise 4 slices,
# not 32. Still ample for one rig; no longer the "32x what this needs" it was at one chip.
#
# WHICH ID GOVERNS WHICH MODEL. Google's Compute Engine provisioning-models page states it:
#   "When you create a Flex-start VM, preemptible quota is consumed. If your project lacks
#    preemptible quota, then standard quota is consumed."
#   https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/provisioning-models
# So FLEX_START spends GCE_SPOT_QUOTA_ID first even though flex-start is not preemptible in
# behaviour (once granted it runs uninterrupted). This rig defaults to flex-start, so the
# preemptible metric is the one that gates it in practice — but a flex-start region is usable
# if EITHER pool has room. Do not write off a region on one listing alone.
#
#   FLEX_START  -> GCE_SPOT_QUOTA_ID first, GCE_QUOTA_ID as documented FALLBACK
#   SPOT        -> GCE_SPOT_QUOTA_ID         (preemptible pool)
#   STANDARD    -> GCE_QUOTA_ID              (regional, family-wide, tpu_family=CT6E)
#
# There is no non-preemptible per-generation v6e id at all, which is why on-demand falls
# back to the generic family quota. v4/v5e/v5p each publish their own pair; v6e does not.
#
# The preemptible id is used at REGION scope: the per-zone spelling
# (PREEMPTIBLE-TPU-V6E-per-project-zone) exists but carries no entries in this project,
# while the per-region one holds the real values — 1536 in most regions, 32 in us-east5,
# 0 in us-east4. Defaults differ between the two ids and this trips people up: a region
# absent from the family quota inherits 0, a region absent from the preemptible quota
# inherits 1536.
#
# Requests denied in us-central1 (family), us-west1 (family) and us-east4 (both) were retried
# at 32 and again at 8 chips on 2026-08-11 — denied every time, within seconds, no partial
# grant. The ask size is not the variable; the region is. Do not retry them, and note that
# 8 was one of the sizes already refused, so this rig's slice size buys no new answer there.
#
# DO NOT RE-REQUEST us-east5 PREEMPTIBLE. An increase from 32 to 1536 was submitted
# 2026-08-11 and came back "Quota request denied" within seconds. Grants are a property of
# the region, not of the number asked for.
GCE_QUOTA_ID=TPUS-PER-TPU-FAMILY-per-project-region
GCE_SPOT_QUOTA_ID=PREEMPTIBLE-TPU-V6E-per-project-region
GCE_TPU_FAMILY=CT6E
# The twin rig's ids, kept only so comparison tooling can report both. Compute Engine
# does not consult them.
TPU_QUOTA_ID=TPUV6EPerProjectPerZoneForTPUAPI
TPU_SPOT_QUOTA_ID=TPUV6EPreemptiblePerProjectPerZoneForTPUAPI

# Name of the instance this rig provisions. On this path the instance IS the node — there is
# no Queued Resource indirection and no derived <id>-node. Left unset it derives from the rig
# directory (gce-vllm-v6e8-31b). Set it only to reach something created under an older name;
# a rename otherwise orphans it and it keeps billing — at eight chips, expensively.
#INSTANCE_NAME=gce-vllm-v6e8-31b
#SERVICE_NAME=gce-vllm-v6e8-31b

# Name this rig's MCP server advertises, and the key the client must register it under —
# that key prefixes every tool, as mcp__<name>__find_tpu. Left unset it derives from the rig
# directory (gce-vllm-v6e8-31b), which is what keeps it distinguishable from the TPU-API twin
# registered as tpu-vllm-v6e8-2b. `make mcp-config` writes a .mcp.json using the same value.
#MCP_SERVER_NAME=gce-vllm-v6e8-31b
